FLUX
====

FLUX is a table access method that updates rows in place and stores old row
versions in UNDO instead of leaving dead tuples in the main relation.  It works
on today's executor APIs and on plain 6-byte-TID indexes, but it does add one
index-AM entry point: nbtree delete-marking (amdeletemark), which is what lets
an indexed-column UPDATE keep the row's TID.  The design follows the zheap
proposal (in-place UPDATE with old versions in UNDO); zheap's README describes
delete-marking, which zheap did not implement.

This README describes FLUX's design and how it differs from heap.  For the
UNDO substrate FLUX relies on, see src/backend/access/undo/README; FLUX writes
its table UNDO to the per-backend engine (am_undo_engine =
UNDO_ENGINE_PERBACKEND, see src/include/access/flux_pbu.h).


In-place UPDATE and UNDO
------------------------

* In-place UPDATE.  The new tuple version overwrites the old one in the same
  slot (same TID).  The old version is pushed to the per-backend undo log via
  the tuple header's version pointer (t_verptr).  A snapshot that cannot see
  the updater reconstructs the old version by walking that UNDO chain
  (flux_pvs.c, FluxReconstructVisibleVersion -> FluxPbuFetchVersion).  So an
  UPDATE that does not change any indexed column touches no index at all, and
  FLUX keeps the TID even when an indexed column does change (see the
  secondary-index section below).

* UNDO-based rollback.  ROLLBACK (and error/abort) replays the undo chain
  backward, restoring before-images in place (flux_undo.c's rm_undo, dispatched
  by execute_undo_actions).  There are no dead tuples to vacuum away for a
  rolled-back change.

* UNDO-based old-version reconstruction for MVCC.  Older snapshots read the
  pre-update image from the UNDO fork, not from a second on-page copy.

* Crash safety.  Every page modification and its UNDO record are WAL-logged
  (resource manager RM_FLUX_ID, flux_xlog.c); recovery replays them and
  per-backend UNDO recovery restores any before-images for transactions that
  were in flight at the crash.

* sLog for uncommitted-writer tracking.  FLUX shares the cluster's sLog
  extension (slog.c, registered once as SLogShmemCallbacks) for self-visibility,
  command-level (CID) checks, and write-write conflict serialization.  The
  authoritative commit oracle is CLOG; visibility is heap-shaped xmin/xmax
  (see below).


How FLUX differs from heap
--------------------------

1. Old versions live in UNDO, not in the table.  Heap keeps every old row
   version in the relation until VACUUM removes it (bloat).  FLUX keeps only
   the newest version on the page and stores the old one in the undo log,
   which is discarded once no snapshot needs it.  A non-in-place change's old
   version and a DELETE's tombstone are reclaimable as soon as the transaction
   commits and the version falls below the removable horizon.

2. In-place for key-changing UPDATEs too, via nbtree delete-marking, with a
   capability gate that falls back to a new TID where in-place index
   maintenance would be unsound.  See the next section.

3. Wide values use standard heap TOAST.  A FLUX relation gets an ordinary
   heap TOAST table (relation_needs_toast_table mirrors heap; relation_toast_am
   returns the heap AM).  On INSERT/UPDATE, oversized varlena columns are
   externalized through the AM-agnostic toast_helper routines (flux_toast_tuple
   in flux_tuple.c) exactly as heap does.  As in heap, a DELETE (also the
   delete half of an out-of-place UPDATE) deletes the external values of the
   version it deletes (flux_toast_delete), as an MVCC delete that commits or
   rolls back with it, and TOAST VACUUM reclaims them; VACUUM of the table
   does not touch TOAST.  FLUX has no on-page overflow mechanism of its own.

4. Heap-shaped xmin/xmax visibility.  FLUX uses the "boring correct"
   visibility model: each tuple carries t_xmin (inserter) and t_xmax
   (deleter/updater), resolved against CLOG and the reader's snapshot exactly
   like HeapTupleSatisfiesMVCC.


Secondary indexes and the key-changing UPDATE
--------------------------------------------

Secondary indexes on a FLUX table are ordinary heap-TID nbtree (or any
standard index-AM) entries: plain 6-byte TIDs, exactly like heap.  No per-tuple
generation and no wider index identity.  FLUX does rely on one index-AM
addition -- nbtree delete-marking (amdeletemark / index_delete_mark) -- which is
what lets it keep the TID across an indexed-column UPDATE.

The hazard an in-place-with-stable-TID AM creates for a plain-TID index is the
"A -> B -> A" problem: if the tuple stays at one TID while an indexed column
changes, the index would accumulate a stale (k_old, TID) entry beside the new
(k_new, TID) entry, both pointing at the same live TID; an index scan would then
double-count or return rows under the wrong key.

FLUX handles it in three cases:

  * An UPDATE that changes NO indexed column is done IN PLACE (same TID).  The
    index entries still point at the right key and TID, so no index
    maintenance is needed.  flux_tuple_update sets *update_indexes = TU_None
    and the executor inserts no index entries.

  * An UPDATE that changes an indexed column, where FLUX can maintain every
    index itself, is ALSO done IN PLACE at the same TID
    (am_index_delete_marking = true).  flux_inplace_index_maintenance inserts
    the new (k_new, TID) entry and delete-marks the old (k_old, TID) entry, and
    reports TU_None so the executor does no index work.  The delete-marked
    entry is kept, not removed, so a snapshot that predates the key change still
    reaches the row under k_old; a scan that arrives through a delete-marked
    entry sets xs_recheck so the executor re-evaluates the qual.  FLUX's own
    table UNDO reverses both halves on rollback (clear the old delete-mark,
    remove the new entry), which is why nbtree/hash suppress their separate
    index UNDO for a delete-marking parent.

  * An UPDATE that changes an indexed column where in-place maintenance would
    NOT be sound falls back to OUT OF PLACE (new TID, TU_All, executor does all
    index work including the constraint checks).  The gate is
    flux_inplace_index_maint_possible(), and it refuses four cases, each of
    which was first observed as a silent wrong answer:
      1. an index AM without delete-marking (only nbtree has it; gist, gin,
         spgist and brin do not) -- otherwise the stale entry stays live and an
         index scan returns a row that does not satisfy the qual;
      2. an EXCLUSION constraint (indisexclusion) -- the check lives in the
         executor, so reporting TU_None would skip it entirely;
      3. a DEFERRABLE constraint (!indimmediate) -- FLUX cannot queue the
         end-of-statement recheck event;
      4. an index that is not valid yet (!indisvalid), which CREATE INDEX
         CONCURRENTLY or REINDEX CONCURRENTLY is building -- the build
         indexes each TID once and its validation adds only missing TIDs, so
         a key changed in place at a TID it already has is never indexed.
         Heap never updates such a row HOT either: the new TID is what the
         build and the validation pick up.
    A SUMMARIZING index (BRIN) is exempt from rule 1: it has no per-row
    (key, TID) entry to delete-mark and a range scan rechecks the table tuple.

  * The same out-of-place fallback is taken when delete-marking cannot
    describe the change in some btree index (flux_index_change_exact): the
    old and new entries must be different keys, since the index tells them
    apart only by its comparison.  That fails when only an INCLUDE column
    changed (same key, so the entry would keep the old INCLUDE values), and
    for any key change in an index whose key columns are not all
    "equalimage" (an opclass that calls different images equal, e.g. numeric
    3 and 3.00).  The rule is per index, from the index's own metadata: the
    opclasses' BTEQUALIMAGE_PROC, which nbtree also uses to decide
    deduplication.

Stock PostgreSQL no longer tells the AM which columns changed, so FLUX
self-computes it (flux_indexed_attr_changed), matching heap's internal
HeapDetermineColumnsInfo: it deforms the old on-page tuple, reads the new
values from the slot, and value-compares every attribute referenced by any
index (RelationGetIndexAttrBitmap HOT_BLOCKING + SUMMARIZED).

Because a delete-marked entry's key may not describe the live tuple, index-only
scans are suppressed for indexes on a delete-marking table (enforced in the
planner).

Consequence: idxscan, seqscan, and bitmapscan return identical results across
any sequence of key-changing UPDATEs (including A -> B -> A recurrences).
Verified by amcheck (heapallindexed) and by the scan cross-check in the test
suite.  Note that amcheck's heapallindexed treats a delete-marked entry whose
key mismatches the table tuple as expected rather than as corruption.


Limitations
-----------

* COPY FREEZE is accepted but does not pre-freeze rows or set visibility-map
  bits on FLUX.  Rows retain ordinary MVCC visibility, so older snapshots
  do not see the new rows as they can with heap's COPY FREEZE.

* Index-only scans are not used on a FLUX table: get_relation_info()
  (plancat.c) sets canreturn to false for every index of a table whose AM
  does delete-marking.  A delete-marked entry left by an in-place key UPDATE
  can carry a key that no longer matches the row until VACUUM removes the
  entry.

* An UPDATE that FLUX cannot do in place -- an indexed-column change refused
  by flux_inplace_index_maint_possible() (exclusion or deferrable constraint,
  an index AM without delete-marking, or an index that is not valid yet), or a new version that does not fit
  on its page -- moves the row to a new TID.  The old version is marked
  FLUX_TUPLE_SUPERSEDED and linked to the new one, as heap's t_ctid chain
  does, so a waiter sees the row as updated rather than deleted.

* An UPDATE that changes only INCLUDE columns of a btree index, or that
  changes the key of a btree index with a key opclass that is not
  "equalimage", moves the row to a new TID too (flux_index_change_exact),
  like heap's non-HOT UPDATE.  Common such opclasses: numeric, float4 and
  float8, interval, jsonb, arrays (anyarray), ranges and multiranges,
  record, tsvector/tsquery, and text or varchar with a nondeterministic
  collation.  Only the key columns count: a key change in an index on (int)
  INCLUDE (numeric) stays in place.  UPDATEs that change no column of such
  an index stay in place.

* Row locks are held in the shared tuple sLog, not on the row.  Its hash
  partitions have a fixed size; a row lock that does not fit fails with
  "could not store row lock in sLog".  A prepared transaction's row locks are
  made durable with a FLUX two-phase record (FLUX_2PC_ROWLOCK).

* VACUUM scans the table, then vacuums each index, then compacts the pages.
  It does not use parallel index vacuuming and does not report progress in
  pg_stat_progress_vacuum.  A non-aggressive VACUUM does not compact a page
  that another backend has pinned (it needs a cleanup lock), as heap's
  lazy_scan_noprune does; an aggressive one waits for the pin.

* While an index of the table is not valid, every UPDATE that changes an
  indexed column moves the row to a new TID (rule 4 above), as heap makes
  such an UPDATE non-HOT.  That lasts for the whole of a CREATE INDEX
  CONCURRENTLY or REINDEX CONCURRENTLY, and indefinitely for an invalid index
  that a failed concurrent build leaves behind, until it is dropped or rebuilt
  with REINDEX.  UPDATEs that change no indexed column stay in place.

* REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) works as for heap, and like heap's it is not
  MVCC-safe: the copy takes the rows the decoding's initial snapshot sees,
  and the changes committed after it are decoded from the WAL (flux_decode)
  and replayed by key onto the new table.  CLUSTER and VACUUM FULL keep the
  row versions that older snapshots still need.

* user_catalog_table is refused on FLUX, at CREATE TABLE ... USING flux, at
  ALTER TABLE ... SET (user_catalog_table = true), and at ALTER TABLE ...
  SET ACCESS METHOD flux on an already-marked heap table
  (CheckFluxUserCatalogTable in tablecmds.c, with a hint to use heap).  An
  output plugin reads a user_catalog_table under a historic snapshot that
  reconstructs a past catalog state from older row versions; FLUX visibility
  has no branch for those historic snapshots (heap's
  HeapTupleSatisfiesHistoricMVCC) and FLUX does not log the command ids
  decoding needs to see catalog rows changed by the transaction being
  decoded, so a FLUX user_catalog_table would silently return wrong rows to
  decoding.  Put logical-decoding plugin metadata in a heap table.  Because
  a FLUX relation can therefore never be a catalog relation,
  RelationIsAccessibleInLogicalDecoding() is always false for FLUX: the
  catalog_rel flag in the FLUX freeze/defrag WAL records is always false and
  the catalog branch of ResolveRecoveryConflictWithSnapshot() is unreachable
  for FLUX records.  The ordinary (non-catalog) snapshot recovery conflicts a
  freeze creates on a standby are still raised (flux_pbu.c, flux_xlog.c).


Registration
------------

* pg_am: amname 'flux', amhandler flux_tableam_handler, amtype 't'
  (FLUX_TABLE_AM_OID = 9316; pg_proc oid 9402).
* WAL resource manager: RM_FLUX_ID (rmgrlist.h), redo/desc/identify/mask in
  flux_xlog.c / fluxdesc.c, logical decoding in decode.c (flux_decode).
* UNDO resource manager: UNDO_RMID_FLUX (=5), FluxUndoRmgrInit
  (undormgrlist.h).  FLUX's table UNDO goes to the per-backend engine
  (am_undo_engine = UNDO_ENGINE_PERBACKEND) and its rm_undo also reverses the
  in-place index maintenance, which is why nbtree/hash write no separate index
  UNDO for a FLUX-parented index.
* Two-phase commit: TWOPHASE_RM_FLUX_ID (=5), flux_twophase_* callbacks.
* Shared memory: FluxMvccShmemCallbacks (subsystemlist.h); row locks are in
  the tuple sLog (SLogTupleLock*), allocated on first use.
* GUC: flux_lazy_uncommitted_clear.

FLUX is always compiled (USE_FLUX) because the catalog and rmgr
lists reference its symbols unconditionally.


Source layout
-------------

  flux_handler.c     table-AM routine table (flux_methods), scans, TOAST hooks
  flux_operations.c  INSERT / DELETE / UPDATE (in-place + out-of-place),
                     in-place index maintenance + its capability gate, VACUUM
  flux_tuple.c       tuple form/deform, flux_toast_tuple / flux_toast_delete
  flux_mvcc.c        heap-shaped xmin/xmax visibility state, shmem
  flux_pvs.c         prior-version reconstruction from the undo chain
  flux_slot.c        FLUX minimal tuple slot
  flux_xlog.c        WAL record types, redo, mask
  flux_undo.c        UNDO_RMID_FLUX resource manager (reverse-apply)
  flux_pbu.c         per-backend UNDO engine glue (insert, fetch, chain)
  flux_lock.c        tuple locking (SELECT FOR UPDATE/SHARE)
  flux_vm.c          visibility map maintenance
  flux_fsm.c         free-space map integration
  flux_stats.c       relation size / density estimation helpers
